  "summary": "Layered like a walk in the woods: the Japanese \"forest girl\" dress philosophy.",
  "description": "Mori kei (\"forest style\") began in 2007 when Choco's mixi community defined the mori girl: layer upon layer of loose natural-fiber clothing — crocheted vests over smock dresses over skirts, warm browns, creams and forest greens, with scarves, brooches and vintage lace — dressing like a storybook wanderer rather than Harajuku's louder tribes. Peak media attention (mooks, Spoon. features) came 2009-2013; the community formalized rules (natural materials, A-line comfort, no glitter) that practitioners then happily broke. Its ethos — slow, handmade, seasonal, self-soothing — prefigured global cottagecore, and mori dressing survives in Japanese slow fashion and worldwide soft-grunge wardrobes.",
  "culturalContext": "Mori kei prefigured slow fashion: the 2007 mixi community's \"forest girl\" layers became Japan's softest street style, its handmade ethos fed global cottagecore, and mori dressing survives in Japanese slow-fashion brands and worldwide comfort-wardrobe culture.",
